{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>That I may test them, whether they will walk in My Torah or not.</b> Rashi explained: whether they will keep the commandments that depend on it — that they not leave over from it and not go out on the Sabbath to gather. And all this was said concerning the trait of trust, for if they do not leave over from it, this is a sign that they are whole in the trait of trust — for whoever has bread in his basket and says, 'What will I eat tomorrow?' is among those of little faith. And likewise if they do not go out on the Sabbath to gather, they are trusting that what they gathered yesterday will suffice them also for the morrow, and then 'they will walk in My Torah' — for one who is not whole in the trait of trust consumes all his days gathering and hoarding, and when will he make for himself a house of HaShem to engage in Torah? As is well known from the ways of most of the world who go after vanity and become vain, and the business of Torah is secondary to them." "Another matter: because the engagement of Torah has impediments from within and from without. From within — that is, on account of coarse foods that spoil the clarity and purity of the intellect, until the power of his intellect passes through a turbid valley and is not pure enough to engage in matters of understanding. For on this account Moses did not eat on the mountain for forty days, so that his intellect would be pure and clean to understand matters of understanding, as it is written (Exodus 24:16) ״וַיְכַסֵּהוּ הֶעָנָן שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 4a) that this was in order to purge the eating and drinking within his intestines. And from without — they were across the Jordan, passing through seas and rivers with feet like gazelles to chase after their sustenance, in the wilderness, on the mountain, in the desert and in the lowland, and this pursuit distracts him from engaging in Torah. And this mann was a spiritual food: all who ate it were saved from these two impediments. From without, for they did not need to go about chasing after it, but each one found his sustenance every day at the entrance of his tent. From within, for it was pure food, clean of all dross, and our Rabbis of blessed memory (ibid. 75b) called it ״לֶחֶם אַבִּירִים״ — that the ministering angels eat it, even though there is no eating among them, nevertheless they are nourished from the radiance of the Shechinah, and that is the same spiritual portion that was in the mann — for there was in it a spark also of spirituality, ״וְעֵינוֹ כְּעֵין הַבְּדֹלַח״ (Numbers 11:7), shining and gleaming, and there was no dross in it at all, and therefore it was absorbed in the limbs. And through this the two impediments were removed. And a third upon them all: one who has far more than he needs — he too is not free to engage in Torah, for the abundance of his wealth and possessions and the satiety of the rich do not leave him to engage in Torah. And from this impediment too they were saved through this mann, for it came down only as the portion of each man, day by day. And HaShem said, ״לְמַעַן אֲנַסֶּנּוּ הֲיֵלֵךְ בְּתוֹרָתִי אִם לֹא״ (Exodus 16:4) — for with the removal of all the impediments, a set table is before them to engage in Torah with nothing holding them back; and if after all this they do not walk in the Torah of HaShem, this is nothing but the evil of the heart that has bent them to spurn the Torah of HaShem, and then surely they will not be worthy to receive the Torah. And this will be explained further nearby."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:53.852883606Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}